\section{A Register Number Does Not Identify a Value}

Suppose two older instructions both write \(x_5\), and a third reads \(x_5\). It needs the value from the younger of the two older instructions, even if the older one has already produced a result and the younger one is still waiting for memory. Forwarding from the older instruction is not a productive shortcut. When the consumer finally runs, it would silently use the wrong operand. The architectural rule is a read-after-write dependence; a hardware hazard occurs when a particular implementation cannot yet satisfy it.

A forwarding path lets a consumer take a value before it reaches the register file, but only if the required producer's value is ready at the point of use. A load may have computed its address and still have nothing useful to forward. A structural hazard is different: independent operations can both be ready yet contend for one port. Write-after-read and write-after-write ordering also need protection if reads or writes are allowed to pass one another; an in-order pipeline with uniformly ordered reads and commits may prevent those cases by construction. The hazard logic must reflect the implemented ordering, not infer it from the mere presence of an instruction name.

\section{Declare a Path, Then Synthesize Its Control}

Here is the forwarding and decode excerpt from Listing~12 of the VADL paper, with typographic spacing normalized. It presupposes the fetch stage and the ISA register file.

\begin{verbatim}
[forwarding]
logic bypass

stage DECODE -> (ir: Instruction) = {
  let instr = decode(FETCH.fr) in {
    instr.readOrForward(@X, @bypass)
    ir := instr
  }
}
\end{verbatim}

The abstraction does not promise that every value can be forwarded. A MiA must provide the relevant producing connections as well, and a stall is still needed when no permitted path can deliver the correct value. Section~3.4.4 discusses using the logic element's semantics in synthesis so that a resolvable dependence need not stall, and describes default control and hazard detection when they are not supplied. Sections~4.7.3--4.7.4 give the ordering of the implementation analysis: assign resource ports, then synthesize logic elements using the pipeline and the program points that supply or consume their signals \cite{himmelbauer2024vadl}. This is a source for control generation, not a declaration of the resulting gates or of a compiler scheduling table.

