\section{Select the Producer Before Asking Whether It Is Ready}

Consider an in-order issue pipeline in which D reads operands, E computes a load address, M obtains the data, and W commits the result. For this example a forwarded M result can be used by D in the \emph{following} cycle; the register file reflects all writes already committed at the start of D's operand selection. The following pseudocode is an illustrative operand rule, not VADL.

\begin{verbatim}
operand(consumer, r, cycle):
    if hardwired(r): return constant(r)
    p = youngest older nonsquashed uncommitted writer of r
    if p is absent: return register_file[r]
    if ready(p, cycle) and has_path(p, consumer, cycle):
        return forwarded_value(p)
    return WAIT                       # never fall back to an older value
\end{verbatim}

Begin with \(x_5=1\), then issue \texttt{addi x5, x0, 7}, \texttt{lw x5, 0(x1)}, and \texttt{addi x6, x5, 1}. The addressed word holds \(42\). In cycle 4, the first instruction is in M, the load is in E, and the consumer first attempts its D read. The first result is \(7\), but the load is the nearest writer and its data are not ready. D stays occupied through cycle 5, when the load is in M. At the start of cycle 6 the data are available on the assumed path, so D reads \(42\) and the consumer later writes \(43\). Whether \(7\) came from a bypass or was already in the register file cannot affect that decision. A flushed writer must be removed from this age order before it can block or forward; conversely, a hardwired zero register normally creates no live writer at all. \Cref{fig:visual-332} isolates the two competing values at the first decision point.

