\section{Give the Compiler a Cost, Not a New Semantics}

A scheduler estimates when each result can feed each kind of use and when a functional unit can accept more work. A useful model distinguishes result latency from how long a resource is reserved and can account for particular bypasses. For the trace above it may put independent arithmetic between load and consumer to fill an otherwise empty issue slot, provided it preserves register and memory dependencies and observable effects. It cannot move the consumer ahead of the load or assume two memory operations are independent merely because the pipeline has two ports. On an interlocked implementation, a pessimistic latency estimate may waste opportunities; an optimistic one may cause hardware stalls rather than change the result. That distinction does not extend to machines whose ISA exposes scheduling obligations.

Section~4.1 identifies scheduling as a consumer of MiA information. The reported cycle experiments in Section~5.7 and hardware comparison in Section~5.8 are not evidence that every synthesized bypass was exported into a validated compiler scheduler. A useful separate test is to run dependent pairs both adjacent and with independent instructions between them: the architectural results should agree, and the cycle traces should reveal whether the chosen schedule hid the intended stall.
